## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of a practical low-level concurrency primitive (SPSC) and memory ordering without locks in a real data-path scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Ring buffer wrap-around and capacity checks
- SPSC invariants: single producer advances tail, single consumer advances head
- Atomic head/tail with acquire/release semantics
- Correct visibility guarantees without heavy synchronization

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for SPSC queue
typedef struct { int data; } Item;
#define CAP 1024
Item buf[CAP];
std::atomic<size_t> head(0), tail(0);

bool enqueue(Item it){
  size_t h = head.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
  size_t t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  if (t - h >= CAP) return false; // full
  buf[t % CAP] = it;
  tail.store(t+1, std::memory_order_release);
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(Item* out){
  size_t h = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  size_t t = tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
  if (t - h == 0) return false; // empty
  *out = buf[h % CAP];
  head.store(h+1, std::memory_order_release);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers without stalls?
- What tests would you add to detect drift or data loss under bursty input?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of NUMA-aware memory management and lock-free structures beyond simple queues. Candidates must justify locality decisions, reclamation delay trade-offs, and fragmentation management under mixed workloads. The prompt also probes API design, minimal synchronization strategies, and practical benchmarking methodology.

## Key Concepts

- **NUMA locality**: Allocations should prefer the local node to avoid expensive remote memory access
- **Slab allocator design**: Pre-allocates memory in fixed-size slabs per object size class
- **Size bucketing**: Groups objects into size classes (32–128B, 1–4KB) to reduce fragmentation
- **Lock-free free lists**: CAS-based singly-linked lists enable concurrent access without heavy locking
- **Cross-node reclamation**: Deferred reclamation via epoch orquEUe avoids false sharing and reduces ping-ponging
- **Alignment and false sharing**: Cache-line padding prevents unrelated objects from sharing the same line
- **TLB/cache effects**: Slab locality improves cache hit rates; large pages (huge TLB entries) reduce TLB misses for bulk operations

